nals.','To split one signal into multiple output signals.','To select one input signal out of several options.','A',12),(3344,'What is the role of a Multiplexer (Mux)?','choose','To select one of several input signals and route it to a single output line.','To route a single input signal to multiple output lines.','To convert binary signals.','To generate a clock signal.','A',12),(3345,'What is the role of a Demultiplexer (Demux)?','choose','To select one input signal out of several options.','To route a single input signal to one of several output lines.','To combine multiple input signals into a single signal.','To perform binary addition.','B',12),(3346,'What is the role of Binary Code Converters?','choose','To convert one type of binary code into another type of binary code.','To generate a clock signal.','To manage the registers in the CPU.','To perform only BCD to binary conversion.','A',12),(3347,'What is the role of Latches and Flip Flops?','choose','To perform arithmetic operations.','To serve as sequential logic circuits for storing and holding binary data.','To serve only as Demultiplexers.','To serve only as Adders.','B',12),(3348,'What is the role of Shift Registers?','choose','To manage access to the main memory.','To shift and store data in a serial manner.','To be used only for parallel data storage.','To perform only addition operations.','B',12),(3349,'What is the characteristic of Asynchronous Counters?','choose','The output changes synchronously with the clock signal.','The output changes asynchronously (in a ripple style).','\"They require complex logic circuits.\"','They are used only to implement a Moore FSM.','B',12),(3350,'What is the difference between Mealy and Moore Machines?','choose','Mealy produces output based only on the current state.','Moore produces output based only on the current input.','Mealy produces output based on both the current state and input, Moore produces output based only on the current state.','There is no difference between them.','C',12),(3351,'What is the characteristic of Synchronous Counters?','choose','The output changes only asynchronously.','The output changes simultaneously with the clock edge.','They are used only to implement a Mealy FSM.','They cannot be implemented using Flip Flops.','B',12),(3352,'What is the goal of State Minimization Techniques in FSM?','choose','To increase the number of states in the machine.','To optimize the number of states by eliminating redundant or unreachable states.','To change the transition function.','To change the output function.','B',12),(3353,'What is the role of Read Only Memory (ROM)?','choose','Storing short-term data.','Storing data that can be modified after initial programming.','Permanent storage of firmware and BIOS.','Storing the results of arithmetic operations.','C',12),(3354,'What is the function of the Programmable Logic Array (PLA)?','choose','Implementing only combinational logic.','Implementing only sequential logic.','Implementing both combinational and sequential logic functions.','Serving only as a memory device.','C',12),(3355,'What does the Instruction Set Architecture (ISA) define?','choose','The user interface of the operating system.','The interface between the hardware and software of a computer system, including instructions, data types, and addressing modes.','The size of the main memory.','The speed of the GPU.','B',12),(3356,'What characterizes Accumulator-based Architecture?','choose','Using a Stack to store operands.','Using a dedicated register (Accumulator) to store intermediate results.','Performing operations directly between registers only.','Using main memory to store intermediate results.','B',12),(3357,'What characterizes Stack-based Architecture?','choose','The CPU uses a Stack to store operands and results during computation.','The CPU uses dedicated registers only.','It is always faster than a register-based architecture.','It requires a larger cache memory.','A',12),(3358,'What characterizes Register-Memory Architecture?','choose','The CPU uses only main memory for data storage.','The CPU uses both registers and main memory for data storage.','Operations are performed directly between two registers.','It is only used in old computers.','B',12),(3359,'What characterizes Register-Register Architecture?','choose','Operations are performed directly between registers, with registers used for both operands and results.','Operations are performed directly between a register and main memory.','It is only used in operating systems.','It is only used in IoT applications.','A',12),(3360,'What is the purpose of Instruction Encoding Techniques?','choose','To determine the format of instructions, opcode, and operands.','To manage bus cycles.','To reduce the number of registers.','To allocate memory.','A',12),(3361,'What does Amdahl\'s Law state?','choose','The upper limit to system speedup is limited by the fraction of the system that is optimized.','The ability to perform infinite parallelism.','That system speedup depends only on the CPU speed.','That system speedup depends only on the memory speed.','A',12),(3362,'What is the purpose of Memory Hierarchy?','choose','To ensure that main memory is always the fastest.','To organize different memory levels (e.g., cache, main, secondary) to optimize speed, capacity, and cost.','To use only Cache Memory.','To prevent the use of registers.','B',12),(3363,'What is the role of Cache Memory?','choose','To serve as secondary storage for the long term.','To store frequently accessed data and instructions to reduce the CPU\'s access time to main memory.','To manage registers in the CPU.','To manage I/O allocation.','B',12),(3364,'What is the main disadvantage of Programmed I/O?','choose','It requires the use of DMA.','It requires the CPU to wait for I/O operations to complete.','It does not allow the CPU to control data transfer.','It uses an Interrupt mechanism.','B',12),(3365,'What is the advantage of Interrupt-Driven I/O?','choose','It allows the CPU to perform other tasks while waiting for I/O operations to complete.','It eliminates the need for a CPU.','It requires the CPU to wait constantly.','It uses DMA only.','A',12),(3366,'What does Direct Memory Access (DMA) enable?','choose','Data transfer directly between the CPU and memory.','Data transfer directly between I/O devices and main memory, without CPU involvement.','Interrupt management only.','Restricting access to main memory.','B',12),(3367,'What is the role of the DMA Controller?','choose','To offload the CPU from the time-consuming task of data transfer.','To manage cache memory.','To generate clock signals.','To perform address decoding.','A',12),(3368,'How is Instruction Level Parallelism (ILP) achieved?','choose','Only by using multi-core processors.','Through techniques like Pipelining and Superscalar Execution.','By using Disk Scheduling.','By limiting the number of instructions in the CPU.','B',8),(3369,'What are the three main types of Pipeline Hazards?','choose','Logic, Control, Arithmetic.','Structural, Data, Control.','Memory, I/O, CPU.','Input, Output, Process.','B',8),(3370,'What does Data Level Parallelism enable?','choose','Performing multiple operations on a single data element simultaneously.','Performing multiple operations on different data elements simultaneously (SIMD/Vector Processing).','Restricting the use of parallelism.','Using only Pipelining.','B',8),(3371,'What is Branch Prediction used for in processors?','choose','To reduce cache memory usage.','To optimize instruction execution in the presence of conditional branches.','For I/O management only.','To perform arithmetic operations.','B',8),(3372,'What characterizes Multiple Issue Architectures (Superscalar)?','choose','Processors capable of executing only one instruction every two clock cycles.','Processors capable of issuing and executing multiple instructions in parallel per clock cycle.','They are used only in Data Level Parallelism applications.','They are limited to Accumulator-based Architecture.','B',8),(3373,'What is a Kernel in an Operating System?','choose','A software application for database management.','The central component of the OS that manages and facilitates communication between hardware and software.','A client-side application for a user interface.','A graphical processor.','B',12),(3374,'What is a Process?','choose','An instance of a program in execution, managed by the operating system.','A dedicated register in the CPU.','A type of cache memory.','A function that calls itself.','A',12),(3375,'What are Threads?','choose','They are always isolated from each other with their own memory space.','Lightweight, independent sequences of instructions within a process that can be executed concurrently.','They are always at the Kernel level only.','They do not share memory space with the parent Process.','B',12),(3376,'What is Deadlock?','choose','A state where one process holds one resource.','A situation where two or more processes or threads wait for each other to release a resource, causing a permanent halt.','A state where a CPU reaches O(log n) complexity.','A network partition state.','B',12),(3377,'What is the role of Scheduling Algorithms in an Operating System?','choose','To determine the order of process/thread execution on the CPU.','To manage user files.','To allocate virtual memory.','To manage I/O only.','A',12),(3378,'What is the role of Memory Management?','choose','Managing secondary storage devices.','Allocating and managing the primary memory (RAM) in a computer system.','Managing I/O only.','Managing computer networks.','B',12),(3379,'What does Secondary Storage Management include?','choose','Managing primary memory only.','Managing non-volatile storage devices like hard drives (file system creation, formatting).','Managing keyboard and mouse I/O.','Managing cache memory.','B',12),(3380,'What does File Management include?','choose','Managing RAM memory.','Organizing, storing, and managing files in a computer system (creation, deletion, permissions).','Managing I/O of network.','Managing multi-core processors.','B',12),(3381,'What does I/O Management include?','choose','Managing primary memory only.','Managing I/O devices (device drivers, data transfer between CPU/memory and devices).','Managing files in the system.','Managing processes in the CPU.','B',12),(3382,'What is the goal of Disk Scheduling?','choose','To ensure the hard drive uses DMA.','To determine the order of servicing disk I/O requests to optimize disk access time and throughput.','To manage the file system.','To manage Virtual Memory.','B',12),(3383,'What is one technique mentioned to handle Deadlock?','choose','Resource allocation, scheduling, and deadlock detection.','Using only user-level threads.','Disabling DMA.','Increasing memory size.','A',12),(3384,'Which of the following is an example of a component of a CPU mentioned in the definition?','choose','The Control Unit.','The Main Memory.','The Hard Disk Drive.','The Network Interface Card.','A',12),(3385,'Which three common JavaScript Frameworks are mentioned?','choose','Django, Ruby on Rails, Flask.','Angular, React, Vue.','ASP.NET, Spring, Laravel.','jQuery, MooTools, Prototype.','B',10),(3386,'What are React Components?','choose','The static files of the application.','The building blocks of a React application, representing different parts of the user interface.','Methods for database management in React.','Hyperparameters for optimization.','B',10),(3387,'What is the difference between Conventional and Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)?','choose','Conventional Programming uses objects as building blocks.','OOP is organized as a sequence of tasks or functions.','Conventional Programming is a procedural approach, and OOP uses objects as fundamental building blocks.','Both support Inheritance.','C',10),(3388,'What does Data Abstraction enable in OOP?','choose','Exposing all implementation details of data types.','Hiding the implementation details of data types and exposing only essential properties and behaviors.','Using only built-in data types.','Avoiding the use of Inheritance.','B',10),(3389,'What is the relationship between Objects, Classes, and Methods in OOP?','choose','A Class is an instance of an Object.','Objects are a template for creating a Class.','Objects are instances of a Class, and Methods are the actions Objects can perform.','Methods are unrelated to Objects.','C',10),(3390,'What are Methods in OOP?','choose','The data encapsulated in the object.','The actions or behaviors that objects of a class can perform.','The initial values of the object.','The memory addresses of the object.','B',10),(3391,'What is the purpose of Constructors?','choose','To perform resource cleanup before an object is destroyed.','To declare Class variables.','They are special methods used to initialize objects of a Class.','They are regular functions with no name.','C',10),(3392,'What is a key characteristic of Constructors?','choose','They have a different name than the class.','They have the same name as the class and do not have a return type.','They are called manually after object creation.','They are used to free memory only.','B',10),(3393,'What is the purpose of Destructors?','choose','To initialize objects at creation time.','They are special methods used to clean up resources before an object is destroyed.','They are always triggered manually.','They must return a Boolean value.','B',10),(3394,'What is a key characteristic of Destructors?','choose','They have a different name than the class.','They have the same name as the class, preceded by a tilde (~), and do not have a return type.','They are called automatically when an object is created.','They must allocate memory.','B',10),(3395,'What does Operator Overloading enable?','choose','Using only the built-in operators of the language.','Assigning different meanings to operators (like +, -) depending on the operands.','Prohibiting the use of operators with objects.','Limiting the use of operators to built-in types only.','B',10),(3396,'What are Class and Function Templates?','choose','Mechanisms for fixing data types.','Features that allow the creation of generic, reusable code that works with different data types.','Methods for triggering Destructors.','Methods for loading data from a database.','B',10),(3397,'What is Inheritance in OOP?','choose','A situation where one Class can inherit properties and behaviors from another Class.','The action of creating a new Object from a Class.','The action of creating a new Method.','The process of destroying objects.','A',10),(3398,'What is Multiple Inheritance?','choose','The ability of a Class to inherit only from one Class.','The ability of a Class to inherit properties and behaviors from more than one parent Class.','Prohibiting a Class from inheriting from any other Class.','The ability to inherit only Methods and not Attributes.','B',10),(3399,'How is Polymorphism achieved in OOP?','choose','By using only static methods.','By treating objects of different classes as if they are of the same type (if they implement the same interface/behavior).','By prohibiting the use of Interfaces.','Only through Function Overloading.','B',10),(3400,'What is Aggregation in OOP?','choose','A relationship where one object contains others, but the containing object must exist along with the contained objects.','A relationship where one object contains others, but the contained objects can exist independently.','A method for data hiding.','A mechanism that allows the creation of a new Object.','B',10),(3401,'What does the Rate of Growth of Algorithm Complexity represent?','choose','The constant ratio of runtime.','Static memory allocation.','How the running time or resource usage of an algorithm increases as input size grows (Big O).','The exact number of iterations.','C',8),(3402,'What is the primary notation used to represent the rate of growth of algorithm complexity?','choose','Theta notation.','Big O notation.','ASCII notation.','JSON notation.','B',8),(3403,'What do Asymptotic Notations describe?','choose','The number of lines in the code.','The upper and/or lower bounds on algorithm complexity.','The exact runtime of the algorithm.','The required memory size.','B',8),(3404,'Which Asymptotic Notation describes the upper bound on worst-case time complexity?','choose','Omega notation.','Theta notation.','Big O notation.','Epsilon notation.','C',8),(3405,'What do Time-Space Trade Offs represent in algorithms?','choose','An inverse relationship between runtime and memory usage (more time = less memory, or vice versa).','The need to always use minimum memory.','The need for minimum runtime only.','The need for runtime to be equal to memory usage.','A',8),(3406,'What is the purpose of Pattern Matching Algorithms?','choose','To find the occurrences of a specific pattern within a larger data sequence.','To efficiently sort data.','To perform arithmetic operations only.','To manage cache memory.','A',8),(3407,'What is an example of a Pattern Matching Algorithm?','choose','Bubble Sort.','Knuth-Morris-Pratt (KMP) algorithm.','Binary Search.','Quick Sort.','B',8),(3408,'Which searching algorithm requires the array to be sorted beforehand?','choose','Linear Search.','Hash-based Search.','Binary Search.','Breadth-First Search.','C',8),(3409,'What does the Bubble Sort algorithm do?','choose','Uses the divide-and-conquer technique.','Compares and swaps adjacent elements repeatedly until the array is sorted.','Selects the smallest element and moves it to its correct position.','Divides the array into sublists of one element.','B',8),(3410,'What is Recursion?','choose','A programming technique where a function calls another function.','A programming technique where a function calls itself in its own definition.','A method for preventing Deadlocks.','A method for file management.','B',8),(3411,'What is the average-case time complexity of Quick-sort?','choose','O(n^2).','O(n log n).','O(n).','O(1).','B',8),(3412,'What is Factorial Calculation?','choose','Finding the sum of all positive integers up to a given integer.','Finding the product of all positive integers from 1 up to a given integer.','Calculating a square root.','Splitting a string into parts.','B',8),(3413,'What is the characteristic of the Fibonacci Series?','choose','Each number is the product of the two preceding numbers.','Each number is the sum of the two preceding numbers, starting from 0 and 1.','A sequence that always starts with 10.','A sequence of prime numbers.','B',8),(3414,'What is one of the common operations on strings?','choose','Matrix multiplication.','Searching, substring extraction, length calculation, and concatenation.','Traversal using BFS.','Creating truth tables.','B',13),(3415,'What is Word Processing?','choose','The process of designing and creating hardware components.','The creation, editing, and formatting of documents containing text.','A quick sorting algorithm.','A communication protocol.','B',13),(3416,'What are One-Dimensional Arrays?','choose','A data structure that stores elements in a non-linear sequence.','A data structure that stores a collection of elements in a linear sequence accessible by an index.','A data structure that contains only pointers.','A data structure that cannot be sorted.','B',9),(3417,'What is the purpose of Multi-Dimensional Arrays?','choose','To represent a linear sequence of data.','To store elements in more than one dimension, such as rows and columns (matrices).','To represent only non-zero elements.','To represent Fibonacci series.','B',9),(3418,'What is the condition for multiplying two matrices?','choose','The number of columns in the first matrix must equal the number of rows in the second matrix.','They must be square matrices.','The number of rows in the first matrix must equal the number of rows in the second matrix.','The sum of the elements must be zero.','A',13),(3419,'What are Sparse Matrices?','choose','Matrices that contain only non-zero elements.','Matrices that contain mostly zero elements.','Matrices that cannot be sorted.','Matrices that contain only integer elements.','B',9),(3420,'Which data structure principle does a Stack implement?','choose','First-In, First-Out (FIFO).','Last-In, First-Out (LIFO).','Random Access.','Sequential Search.','B',9),(3421,'Which of the following is an operation on a Stack?','choose','Enqueue.','Pop.','Peek (from the middle).','Traverse.','B',9),(3422,'Which data structure principle does a Queue implement?','choose','Last-In, First-Out (LIFO).','First-In, First-Out (FIFO).','Random Access.','Sequential Search.','B',9),(3423,'Which of the following is an operation on a Queue?','choose','Push.','Dequeue.','Peek (from the middle).','Sort.','B',9),(3424,'What characterizes Polish Notation (Prefix Notation)?','choose','Operators are placed after the operands.','Operands appear before the operators.','Operators are placed before the operands.','It requires extensive use of parentheses.','C',13),(3425,'What is the characteristic of Deques (Double-Ended Queue)?','choose','Allows elements to be added or removed from only one end.','Allows elements to be added or removed from both ends (front and rear).','Implements only the LIFO principle.','Implements only the FIFO principle.','B',9),(3426,'Which element is removed first from a Priority Queue?','choose','The element that was inserted first.','The element that was inserted last.','The element with the highest or lowest priority.','The element chosen randomly.','C',9),(3427,'What is the role of Adders in a computer system?','choose','Performing logical operations only.','Performing the addition of binary numbers.','Converting from binary code to decimal.','Managing cache memory.','B',12),(3428,'What is the role of Decoders?','choose','To convert encoded input data into a set of output signals (e.g., address decoding).','To convert output signals into encoded input signals.','To perform sorting operations.','To manage the clock cycle.','A',12),(3429,'What is the role of Encoders?','choose','To convert input signals into an encoded output representation.','To convert output signals into unencoded input signals.','To split one signal into multiple output signals.','To select one input signal out of several options.','A',12),(3430,'What is the role of a Multiplexer (Mux)?','choose','To select one of several input signals and route it to a single output line.','To route a single input signal to multiple output lines.','To convert binary signals.','To generate a clock signal.','A',12),(3431,'What is the role of a Demultiplexer (Demux)?','choose','To select one input signal out of several options.','To route a single input signal to one of several output lines.','To combine multiple input signals into a single signal.','To perform binary addition.','B',12),(3432,'What is the role of Binary Code Converters?','choose','To convert one type of binary code into another type of binary code.','To generate a clock signal.','To manage the registers in the CPU.','To perform only BCD to binary conversion.','A',12),(3433,'What is the role of Latches and Flip Flops?','choose','To perform arithmetic operations.','To serve as sequential logic circuits for storing and holding binary data.','To serve only as Demultiplexers.','To serve only as Adders.','B',12),(3434,'What is the role of Shift Registers?','choose','To manage access to the main memory.','To shift and store data in a serial manner.','To be used only for parallel data storage.','To perform only addition operations.','B',12),(3435,'What is the characteristic of Asynchronous Counters?','choose','The output changes synchronously with the clock signal.','The output changes asynchronously (in a ripple style).','They require complex logic circuits.','They are used only to implement a Moore FSM.','B',12),(3436,'What is the difference between Mealy and Moore Machines?','choose','Mealy produces output based only on the current state.','Moore produces output based only on the current input.','Mealy produces output based on both the current state and input, Moore produces output based only on the current state.','There is no difference between them.','C',12),(3437,'What is the characteristic of Synchronous Counters?','choose','The output changes only asynchronously.','The output changes simultaneously with the clock edge.','They are used only to implement a Mealy FSM.','They cannot be implemented using Flip Flops.','B',12),(3438,'What is the goal of State Minimization Techniques in FSM?','choose','To increase the number of states in the machine.','To optimize the number of states by eliminating redundant or unreachable states.','To change the transition function.','To change the output function.','B',12),(3439,'What is the role of Read Only Memory (ROM)?','choose','Storing short-term data.','Storing data that can be modified after initial programming.','Permanent storage of firmware and BIOS.','Storing the results of arithmetic operations.','C',12),(3440,'What is the function of the Programmable Logic Array (PLA)?','choose','Implementing only combinational logic.','Implementing only sequential logic.','Implementing both combinational and sequential logic functions.','Serving only as a memory device.','C',12),(3441,'What does the Instruction Set Architecture (ISA) define?','choose','The user interface of the operating system.','The interface between the hardware and software of a computer system, including instructions, data types, and addressing modes.','The size of the main memory.','The speed of the GPU.','B',12),(3442,'What characterizes Accumulator-based Architecture?','choose','Using a Stack to store operands.','Using a dedicated register (Accumulator) to store intermediate results.','Performing operations directly between registers only.','Using main memory to store intermediate results.','B',12),(3443,'What characterizes Stack-based Architecture?','choose','The CPU uses a Stack to store operands and results during computation.','The CPU uses dedicated registers only.','It is always faster than a register-based architecture.','It requires a larger cache memory.','A',12),(3444,'What characterizes Register-Memory Architecture?','choose','The CPU uses only main memory for data storage.','The CPU uses both registers and main memory for data storage.','Operations are performed directly between two registers.','It is only used in old computers.','B',12),(3445,'What characterizes Register-Register Architecture?','choose','Operations are performed directly between registers, with registers used for both operands and results.','Operations are performed directly between a register and main memory.','It is only used in operating systems.','It is only used in IoT applications.','A',12),(3446,'What is the purpose of Instruction Encoding Techniques?','choose','To determine the format of instructions, opcode, and operands.','To manage bus cycles.','To reduce the number of registers.','To allocate memory.','A',12),(3447,'What does Amdahl\'s Law state?','choose','The upper limit to system speedup is limited by the fraction of the system that is optimized.','The ability to perform infinite parallelism.','That system speedup depends only on the CPU speed.','That system speedup depends only on the memory speed.','A',12),(3448,'What is the purpose of Memory Hierarchy?','choose','To ensure that main memory is always the fastest.','To organize different memory levels (e.g., cache, main, secondary) to optimize speed, capacity, and cost.','To use only Cache Memory.','To prevent the use of registers.','B',12),(3449,'What is the role of Cache Memory?','choose','To serve as secondary storage for the long term.','To store frequently accessed data and instructions to reduce the CPU\'s access time to main memory.','To manage registers in the CPU.','To manage I/O allocation.','B',12),(3450,'What is the main 