','A pattern for army games.','A',10),(3703,'What is the time complexity of the Ford-Fulkerson algorithm for Maximum Flow?','choose','O(V E^2)','O(E * max_flow)','O(V^3)','O(E log V)','B',8),(3704,'Which of the following problems is known to be NP-Complete?','choose','Minimum Spanning Tree','Shortest Path in weighted graph','Boolean Satisfiability Problem (SAT)','Linear Programming','C',8),(3705,'What is the main difference between P and NP complexity classes?','choose','P problems are hard, NP are easy.','P problems can be solved in polynomial time; NP problems\' solutions can be verified in polynomial time.','P stands for Polynomial, NP stands for Non-Polynomial.','NP problems cannot be solved by computers.','B',8),(3706,'In Dynamic Programming, what is the \'Optimal Substructure\' property?','choose','A problem can be broken down into independent subproblems.','An optimal solution to the problem contains optimal solutions to its subproblems.','The problem requires a greedy approach.','The problem has no overlapping subproblems.','B',8),(3707,'What is the worst-case time complexity of the Edmonds-Karp algorithm?','choose','O(V E^2)','O(E * max_flow)','O(V^3)','O(V^2 E)','A',8),(3708,'Which algorithm is used to find the strongly connected components of a graph in linear time?','choose','Kosaraju\'s Algorithm','Kruskal\'s Algorithm','Prim\'s Algorithm','Bellman-Ford Algorithm','A',8),(3709,'What is the maximum height of a Red-Black Tree with n internal nodes?','choose','2 * log(n+1)','n','log n','n/2','A',9),(3710,'In a B-Tree of order m, what is the maximum number of children a node can have?','choose','m','m-1','m/2','2m','A',9),(3711,'What is the primary use of a Fibonnaci Heap over a Binary Heap?','choose','It has better amortized running time for some operations (like Decrease-Key).','It uses less memory.','It is easier to implement.','It is always faster for all operations.','A',9),(3712,'What is the time complexity for \'Union\' operation in a Disjoint Set with Union by Rank and Path Compression?','choose','O(log n)','O(n)','Inverse Ackermann function (nearly constant).','O(1)','C',9),(3713,'Which data structure is most efficient for storing a dictionary of words for prefix-based search (autocomplete)?','choose','Hash Table','Binary Search Tree','Trie (Prefix Tree)','Graph','C',9),(3714,'What is the advantage of using a splay tree?','choose','It is perfectly balanced.','Recently accessed elements are moved to the root, optimizing for locality of reference.','It has O(1) search time.','It uses no pointers.','B',9),(3715,'What is the \'Dining Philosophers Problem\' an illustration of?','choose','Memory management efficiency.','Synchronization issues and deadlock handling in concurrent systems.','CPU scheduling fairness.','Network routing protocols.','B',12),(3716,'What is the purpose of a \'Translation Lookaside Buffer\' (TLB) miss handling?','choose','To restart the computer.','To fetch the page table entry from main memory into the TLB.','To delete the page.','To switch user mode.','B',12),(3717,'In the context of deadlock avoidance, what is a \'Safe State\'?','choose','A state where no process is running.','A state where there is at least one sequence of process execution that avoids deadlock.','A state with no memory fragmentation.','A state where all resources are free.','B',12),(3718,'What is \'Priority Inversion\'?','choose','When a high-priority task is preempted by a lower-priority task holding a shared resource.','When priorities are reversed by the scheduler randomly.','When the OS crashes.','When a low-priority task runs faster.','A',12),(3719,'What mechanism is used to solve Priority Inversion?','choose','Priority Inheritance Protocol.','Round Robin Scheduling.','Killing the low priority process.','Increasing memory.','A',12),(3720,'What is the \'Flyweight\' Design Pattern used for?','choose','To minimize memory usage by sharing as much data as possible with similar objects.','To create complex objects.','To 